								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2854.msg32694#msg32694"><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2854.msg32694#msg32694">Sonics off-season talk post</a></b>
									<div class="smalltext">&#171; <b> on:</b> July 03, 2007, 11:48:39 am &#187;</div></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;"><a href="http&#58;//seattletimes.nwsource.com/html/sports/2003772733_weblewis03.html" target="_blank">http://seattletimes.nwsource.com/html/sports/2003772733_weblewis03.html</a><br /><br />Lewis agrees to deal with Orlando<br /><br />By Percy Allen<br />Seattle Times staff reporter<br /><br />Free-agent forward Rashard Lewis, who has played his entire nine-year NBA career in Seattle, is headed to Orlando after agreeing to a long-term deal with the Magic.<br /><br />&quot;He had a comfort and a gut feeling and he feels they have a championship core, and that&#039;s what he wants to do is win a championship,&quot; Lewis&#039; agent Tony Dutt told the Associated Press this morning.<br /><br />An Orlando television station, WKMG Local 6, reported that Lewis agreed to a five-year, &#036;75 million contract, however, Dutt said the details are still being worked out. It&#039;s believed Lewis will receive a salary next season in the range of &#036;15 million and with annual eight-percent raises, the total value of his deal could exceed &#036;88 million.<br /><br />There&#039;s a chance Orlando will pursue a sign-and-trade deal with Seattle to ease the burden of its financial commitments. The Sonics can offer Lewis a six-year deal.<br /><br />Players can&#039;t sign contracts until July 11, which gives Seattle a week to try to change Lewis&#039; mind, but a person familiar with the situation said he will not play in Seattle again.<br /><br />&quot;That he&#039;s going to Orlando doesn&#039;t change the fact that he had good years in Seattle,&quot; Dutt said.<br /><br />Reached on Monday night, Sonics general manager Sam Presti acknowledged the reports, but declined to comment.<br /><br />When Lewis opted out of the final two years of a contract that would have paid him &#036;21 million, he said he wanted to avoid drawn-out negotiations, which he believed led to him signing two below-market deals with the Sonics. He fired agent Carl Poston and hired Dutt, who promised Lewis would be among the first of the free agents to reach a deal.<br /><br />Sunday in Houston, Lewis and Dutt entertained a contingent of Sonics front-office executives, including Presti. The team, however, did not make a financial offer, but chose to lay out how the one-time All-Star might fit on a team that appears to be building around first-round draft picks Kevin Durant and Jeff Green.<br /><br />Dutt described the meeting as &quot;professional,&quot; but he and Lewis flew to Orlando on Sunday and met with Magic GM Otis Smith on Monday.<br /><br />Orlando targeted as Lewis as its No. 1 priority in free agency and wanted to pair him with center Dwight Howard. The Magic, however, was believed to be lacking the salary-cap space to give Lewis the contract he wanted. Orlando apparently will allow center Darko Milicic to become an unrestricted free agent.<br /><br />&quot;[Lewis] feels like they&#039;re going to be good for a long time,&quot; Dutt said. &quot;It was important knowing that Howard was going to be around a long time as well.&quot;<br /><br />The Sonics could have offered Lewis more than the Magic, but they were not prepared to pay an average of &#036;15 million per year for the 6-foot-10 forward, who will turn 28 in August.<br /><br />Playing in just 60 games because of a hand injury, Lewis averaged a career-high 22.4 points last season. He also moved ahead of Shawn Kemp into fourth place on the team&#039;s all-time scoring list (10,148 points) and climbed over Nate McMillan for sixth place in rebounding (3,222).<br /><br />Last week the Sonics traded seven-time All-Star guard Ray Allen to Boston for the No. 5 pick, which led to Green, guard Delonte West and Wally Sczcerbiak coming to Seattle.<br /><br />Percy Allen: 206-464-2278 or <a href="mailto:pallen@seattletimes.com">pallen@seattletimes.com</a>.</div>
